WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.800
President Biden

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.201
and Speaker

00:00:01.201 --> 00:00:02.235
McCarthy last night

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:03.169
announced that they have

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:05.438
struck an agreement in principle

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.740
to lift the nation's

00:00:06.740 --> 00:00:08.742
borrowing cap and avert a looming

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:10.043
fiscal catastrophe.

00:00:10.043 --> 00:00:10.844
With the US set

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.679
to run out of money to pay its bills

00:00:12.679 --> 00:00:14.147
as early as Monday, June 5th.

00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:17.317
But a big question remains can they now

00:00:17.317 --> 00:00:20.020
get this deal passed in time?

00:00:20.453 --> 00:00:21.988
President Biden acknowledged last night

00:00:21.988 --> 00:00:22.589
that the agreement

00:00:22.589 --> 00:00:23.790
is, quote, a compromise,

00:00:23.790 --> 00:00:25.158
which means not everyone gets

00:00:25.158 --> 00:00:26.659
what they want, unquote.

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:27.293
And already

00:00:27.293 --> 00:00:28.628
we know about deep concerns

00:00:28.628 --> 00:00:30.830
from progressives and from conservatives.

00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:32.632
McCarthy, on a call

00:00:32.632 --> 00:00:33.833
with Republicans last night

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:34.501
reportedly said

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.236
the Democrats did not get a single thing

00:00:36.236 --> 00:00:37.604
they wanted in the deal.

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:39.672
And McCarthy emphasized what the GOP

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:40.473
got in exchange

00:00:40.473 --> 00:00:41.674
for raising the debt limit,

00:00:41.674 --> 00:00:43.977
capping non-defense domestic spending,

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.546
canceling billions in IRS funding,

00:00:46.746 --> 00:00:48.481
and temporarily imposing work

00:00:48.481 --> 00:00:49.983
requirements on childless

00:00:49.983 --> 00:00:51.217
able bodied adults

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:54.387
younger than 55 who receive food stamps.

00:00:54.854 --> 00:00:55.755
President Biden says

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:56.589
the agreement protects

00:00:56.589 --> 00:00:57.690
Democratic priorities

00:00:57.690 --> 00:00:58.858
and legislative accomplishments.

00:00:58.858 --> 00:01:01.027
One could also note that the deal lifts

00:01:01.027 --> 00:01:01.795
the nation's debt

00:01:01.795 --> 00:01:02.695
limit for two years,

00:01:02.695 --> 00:01:04.197
avoiding a messy fight

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:06.466
just ahead of the 2024 election.

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:09.102
And in general, the cuts are not as deep

00:01:09.102 --> 00:01:09.903
as many House

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:10.937
Republicans wanted

00:01:10.937 --> 00:01:13.006
and many House Democrats feared.

00:01:13.006 --> 00:01:13.406
Joining us

00:01:13.406 --> 00:01:14.407
now, a Republican leader

00:01:14.407 --> 00:01:15.341
who helped negotiate

00:01:15.341 --> 00:01:16.576
this deal, Congressman

00:01:16.576 --> 00:01:18.344
Dustin Johnson of South Dakota.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:18.745
Congressman

00:01:18.745 --> 00:01:19.813
thanks so much for joining us.

00:01:19.813 --> 00:01:20.980
So what can you tell us

00:01:20.980 --> 00:01:22.082
about the specifics

00:01:22.082 --> 00:01:23.083
of what's in this agreement

00:01:23.083 --> 00:01:24.884
and why do you think it's a good deal

00:01:24.884 --> 00:01:26.753
for House Republicans to vote for?

00:01:26.753 --> 00:01:28.088
Yeah, I think you did a good job

00:01:28.088 --> 00:01:29.355
of providing the outlines

00:01:29.355 --> 00:01:30.290
as far as the spending.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:32.592
I mean, this is going to cut non defense

00:01:32.592 --> 00:01:33.560
and non VA

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.895
spending back to 20, 22 levels.

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.831
That is a big get for Republicans.

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:39.933
That's what we had in limits, save, grow,

00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:42.802
and it's going to save $1.5 trillion

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:44.471
over the course of the next ten years.

00:01:44.471 --> 00:01:45.805
And also for six years

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:48.141
establishes caps at 1%

00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:50.076
so we can slow the growth of spending.

00:01:50.076 --> 00:01:51.511
But two things I want to hit

00:01:51.511 --> 00:01:53.012
that your graphic did not have.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:53.780
First off.

00:01:53.780 --> 00:01:55.014
Late last night

00:01:55.014 --> 00:01:56.382
a new provision was agreed

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:57.817
to by the White House

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:58.818
and the speaker

00:01:58.818 --> 00:02:00.386
whereby we're going to unlock

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:01.254
American energy.

00:02:01.254 --> 00:02:03.690
We are going to provide shot clocks

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:04.524
for NEPA review,

00:02:04.524 --> 00:02:05.525
environmental review

00:02:05.525 --> 00:02:07.694
of 12 months and 24 months

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:08.361
that is going to help.

00:02:08.361 --> 00:02:09.762
Whether you like renewable energy

00:02:09.762 --> 00:02:10.697
or traditional energy.

00:02:10.697 --> 00:02:12.632
This is going to help unlock that energy

00:02:12.632 --> 00:02:13.967
to speed up the process by meet

00:02:13.967 --> 00:02:14.601
up with France,

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:16.603
Germany and France, the

00:02:16.603 --> 00:02:17.370
same kind of project

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:18.605
they would get done in two years.

00:02:18.605 --> 00:02:20.373
It takes us seven years. Jake.

00:02:20.373 --> 00:02:22.075
So we do need reform here.

00:02:22.075 --> 00:02:23.810
And obviously, Democrats like Buddha,

00:02:23.810 --> 00:02:25.111
JeJe and Manchin have talked

00:02:25.111 --> 00:02:25.845
about doing this.

00:02:25.845 --> 00:02:27.580
Well, now we're going to get it done.

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.482
And then finally, just a big thing.

00:02:29.482 --> 00:02:31.351
It's administrative PAYGO.

00:02:31.351 --> 00:02:32.519
When you have the administration

00:02:32.519 --> 00:02:33.219
step forward

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:33.753
and propose

00:02:33.753 --> 00:02:35.421
some vast new regulation

00:02:35.421 --> 00:02:36.089
that's going to cost

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:37.757
hundreds of billions of dollars

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:39.626
Now they have to go find the money

00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:41.761
within the existing bureaucracy.

00:02:41.761 --> 00:02:44.397
It is a huge strike against

00:02:44.397 --> 00:02:46.466
a growing regulatory state.

00:02:46.466 --> 00:02:46.933
Yeah, that's

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:47.433
one of the things

00:02:47.433 --> 00:02:47.767
that members

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.035
of Congress complain about the most,

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:50.503
not just with Democratic administrations,

00:02:50.503 --> 00:02:51.304
but Republicans

00:02:51.304 --> 00:02:53.673
passing a law and not allocating money

00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.308
to make sure the law goes through.

00:02:55.308 --> 00:02:56.576
We're hearing a lot of grumbling

00:02:56.576 --> 00:02:58.578
from conservatives in your caucus.

00:02:58.845 --> 00:02:59.579
Congressman Ken

00:02:59.579 --> 00:03:01.514
Buck called this deal

00:03:01.514 --> 00:03:03.316
a debt ceiling surrender.

00:03:03.316 --> 00:03:04.050
Congressman Ralph

00:03:04.050 --> 00:03:05.818
Norman said it was insanity.

00:03:05.818 --> 00:03:07.153
Congressman Bob, good

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:07.620
tweeted that

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.054
no one

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.289
claiming to be a conservative

00:03:09.289 --> 00:03:11.891
could justify a yes vote.

00:03:11.925 --> 00:03:13.860
Their basic criticism

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:14.861
is that

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:15.662
McCarthy gave up

00:03:15.662 --> 00:03:16.329
too much

00:03:16.329 --> 00:03:18.464
and could have could have gotten more.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:20.633
How do you what do you say to that?

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:21.100
Well,

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:23.703
I'm the head of a group of 75

00:03:23.703 --> 00:03:24.804
pragmatic conservatives

00:03:24.804 --> 00:03:26.472
called the Main Street Caucus

00:03:26.472 --> 00:03:27.240
And so when we say

00:03:27.240 --> 00:03:28.575
conservatives are against it,

00:03:28.575 --> 00:03:29.776
I want to make it clear,

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:30.677
I don't know a single

00:03:30.677 --> 00:03:31.778
one of the mainstream

00:03:31.778 --> 00:03:32.712
caucus, conservative

00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:33.947
caucus conservatives.

00:03:33.947 --> 00:03:34.147
Well,

00:03:34.147 --> 00:03:34.981
and even some of them

00:03:34.981 --> 00:03:36.115
I listen, there will be Freedom

00:03:36.115 --> 00:03:38.017
Caucus people who vote for this package.

00:03:38.017 --> 00:03:38.818
So when you're saying

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:40.720
that conservatives have concerns,

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:41.955
it is really

00:03:41.955 --> 00:03:43.957
the most colorful conservatives.

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:45.058
Some of those guys you mentioned

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:46.292
didn't vote for the thing

00:03:46.292 --> 00:03:48.161
when it was kind of a Republican

00:03:48.161 --> 00:03:50.496
wish list, limited, save, grow.

00:03:50.496 --> 00:03:52.632
Those votes were never really in play.

00:03:52.832 --> 00:03:53.866
We get that.

00:03:53.866 --> 00:03:55.368
But overwhelmingly,

00:03:55.368 --> 00:03:56.569
Republicans in this conference

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:57.570
are going to support the deal.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:03:58.705
How could they not?

00:03:58.705 --> 00:04:00.240
It is a fantastic deal.

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:00.840
How many votes

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:01.908
do you think you're going to get?

00:04:01.908 --> 00:04:03.509
How many votes can you afford to lose?

00:04:04.444 --> 00:04:06.746
We're starting the whipping process now.

00:04:06.746 --> 00:04:08.615
I have talked to maybe between two

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:10.683
and three dozen Republican members.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:11.417
I have not heard

00:04:11.417 --> 00:04:12.485
they had a single one of them.

00:04:12.485 --> 00:04:14.187
Tell me I can't support that.

00:04:14.187 --> 00:04:14.587
I just

00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:15.688
I just gave you the names of three

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:16.556
that you can call maybe.

00:04:16.556 --> 00:04:18.391
Well, Buck Norman and good

00:04:18.391 --> 00:04:19.859
to have some thoughts.

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:20.326
I'm not sure.

00:04:20.326 --> 00:04:21.561
Evan Ducey Johnson called Bob

00:04:21.561 --> 00:04:23.363
Good is the perfect way to get his vote.

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:24.264
But and let's be honest,

00:04:24.264 --> 00:04:25.832
Bob, Good will not vote for this thing.

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:26.633
And it doesn't matter

00:04:26.633 --> 00:04:28.501
if Mother Theresa came back from the dead

00:04:28.501 --> 00:04:30.703
and called him. He's not voting for it.

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:32.939
He was never going to win.

00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:34.073
This is going to pass.

00:04:34.073 --> 00:04:35.742
So 70 members of the Main Street

00:04:35.742 --> 00:04:37.010
Caucus that you mentioned,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:37.910
are they all going to vote for it,

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:39.779
you think? I would be surprised.

00:04:39.779 --> 00:04:40.113
I mean,

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:40.413
I haven't

00:04:40.413 --> 00:04:41.481
talked to every single one of them,

00:04:41.481 --> 00:04:42.582
but everybody I'm

00:04:42.582 --> 00:04:44.684
talking to, Jake, understands

00:04:44.684 --> 00:04:46.352
that when you're reducing spending,

00:04:46.352 --> 00:04:47.720
that when you're peeling back

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.322
the regulatory state,

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:51.024
when you are unlocking American energy,

00:04:51.024 --> 00:04:52.892
when you're getting people back to work,

00:04:52.892 --> 00:04:54.360
this is a big deal.

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:55.628
What concessions to McCarthy

00:04:55.628 --> 00:04:56.929
make to Biden and Democrats

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:58.531
to get it across the finish line?

00:04:58.531 --> 00:05:01.367
That is kind of the amazing part to me.

00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:04.370
There were no wins for Democrats

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:06.472
If you look at the state of policy

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:07.840
today in this country

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:08.508
and you say, okay,

00:05:08.508 --> 00:05:09.475
we're going into a deal

00:05:09.475 --> 00:05:10.276
and one side is going to get

00:05:10.276 --> 00:05:10.743
half of the other

00:05:10.743 --> 00:05:11.844
side is going to get half.

00:05:11.844 --> 00:05:13.279
Republicans have pulled their half

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.181
this way. Democrats have pulled that.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.616
They're half of that way.

00:05:16.616 --> 00:05:17.650
There is nothing

00:05:17.650 --> 00:05:19.052
after the passage of this bill

00:05:19.052 --> 00:05:20.253
that would be more liberal

00:05:20.253 --> 00:05:22.522
or more progressive than it is today.

00:05:22.889 --> 00:05:24.123
It's a remarkable

00:05:24.123 --> 00:05:25.591
conservative accomplishment.

00:05:25.591 --> 00:05:25.925
Do you.

00:05:25.925 --> 00:05:26.559
Are you going to need

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:27.560
Democratic votes to get it

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:28.561
passed in the House?

00:05:28.561 --> 00:05:29.962
There will be some Democrats

00:05:29.962 --> 00:05:30.797
will vote for this.

00:05:30.797 --> 00:05:31.564
But what you need them?

00:05:31.564 --> 00:05:32.398
I guess that's my point.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:33.032
Well,

00:05:33.166 --> 00:05:35.268
we only have 222 Republicans, right?

00:05:35.301 --> 00:05:36.736
I think it is certainly plausible

00:05:36.736 --> 00:05:38.571
that we could get 218,

00:05:38.571 --> 00:05:39.339
although I think it's going

00:05:39.339 --> 00:05:40.973
to look a lot better for this country

00:05:40.973 --> 00:05:42.608
if we can put a big number up

00:05:42.608 --> 00:05:43.476
on the board

00:05:43.476 --> 00:05:45.011
Democrats like Joe Biden

00:05:45.011 --> 00:05:46.045
and some in the House

00:05:46.045 --> 00:05:46.646
coming together

00:05:46.646 --> 00:05:48.081
with Republicans to pass this,

00:05:48.081 --> 00:05:48.781
that's going to be better

00:05:48.781 --> 00:05:49.415
for the country.

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:50.550
So Congresswoman

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:51.584
Jayapal, the head of the House

00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:53.619
Progressive Caucus, a Democrat

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:54.721
you know, vote, too.

00:05:54.721 --> 00:05:55.621
Well, I don't know what

00:05:55.621 --> 00:05:56.956
she's going to vote, but but,

00:05:56.956 --> 00:05:57.690
you know, one question

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:58.791
I'm sure is on her mind,

00:05:58.791 --> 00:05:59.325
which is

00:05:59.325 --> 00:06:00.560
why was it so important

00:06:00.560 --> 00:06:00.893
for House

00:06:00.893 --> 00:06:01.794
Republicans

00:06:01.794 --> 00:06:03.129
to have this work requirement

00:06:03.129 --> 00:06:04.764
for able bodied

00:06:04.831 --> 00:06:05.765
food stamp recipients

00:06:05.765 --> 00:06:06.799
who don't have dependents,

00:06:06.799 --> 00:06:08.634
but not important to get

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:10.503
corporations to

00:06:10.503 --> 00:06:12.038
at least pay some taxes

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.706
if they made a profit

00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:14.340
Well, we raised

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:16.142
taxes said not me, but Joe

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.176
Biden and Democrats

00:06:17.176 --> 00:06:18.244
raised taxes

00:06:18.244 --> 00:06:19.278
on corporations

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:21.481
by $700 billion last year.

00:06:22.348 --> 00:06:23.449
There was not, frankly,

00:06:23.449 --> 00:06:25.017
an appetite among Republicans

00:06:25.017 --> 00:06:27.387
to allow Democrats to do that again.

00:06:27.687 --> 00:06:28.821
What there is an appetite for

00:06:28.821 --> 00:06:30.323
is making sure that we're helping people

00:06:30.323 --> 00:06:32.158
find that pathway out of poverty.

00:06:32.158 --> 00:06:33.960
I grew up in a family of modest means.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:35.995
I saw my parents busting their butts

00:06:35.995 --> 00:06:37.363
every single day.

00:06:37.363 --> 00:06:38.965
And I know it was those efforts

00:06:38.965 --> 00:06:40.400
that helped me escape poverty.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.668
You cannot escape poverty without work.

00:06:43.069 --> 00:06:43.803
You just can't.

00:06:43.803 --> 00:06:45.671
It's got to be a part of the solution.

00:06:45.671 --> 00:06:47.840
These requirements are not mean.

00:06:47.840 --> 00:06:48.641
They're not onerous.

00:06:48.641 --> 00:06:49.575
It's 20 hours a week.

00:06:49.575 --> 00:06:50.943
Work, training,

00:06:50.943 --> 00:06:52.812
education, or volunteering

00:06:52.812 --> 00:06:54.747
at a local food bank

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:56.149
for people who are able-bodied,

00:06:56.149 --> 00:06:56.883
not pregnant,

00:06:56.883 --> 00:06:58.785
don't have kids at home, live in an area

00:06:58.785 --> 00:07:00.319
where there are jobs.

00:07:00.319 --> 00:07:01.788
We know they work

00:07:01.788 --> 00:07:03.055
these requirements have been in place

00:07:03.055 --> 00:07:04.624
since 1996

00:07:04.624 --> 00:07:06.058
when Bill Clinton signed them into law.

